== Lecture 12 · Unique factorization and irreducibility
#focus[Distinguish irreducible from prime, prove unique factorization in PIDs, and use Gauss's lemma and reduction tests.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall irreducibility forbids factorizations, while primality controls divisibility of products.


#definition[Factorization vocabulary][In a domain, nonzero nonunits $p$ are *irreducible* if $p=a b$ forces a factor to be a unit, and *prime* if $p|a b$ forces $p|a$ or $p|b$. Elements differing by a unit are associates. A unique factorization domain (UFD) admits factorizations into irreducibles, unique up to order and associates.]

#theorem[PIDs are UFDs][Every PID is a UFD.]
#strategy[First prove irreducibles are prime using Bézout. Then rule out infinite failure of factorization using increasing ideals.]
#proof[If irreducible $p$ does not divide $a$, a gcd of $p,a$ is a unit, so $u p+v a=1$. If $p|a b$, multiply this identity by $b$ to obtain $p|b$. For existence, a PID satisfies the ascending chain condition on ideals: the union of an increasing chain is an ideal $(d)$, and $d$ belongs to one member, so the chain stabilizes there. If a nonunit had no finite irreducible factorization, split it into two nonunits and choose a factor still lacking a factorization. Repetition would give an infinite strictly increasing chain of principal ideals, a contradiction. Uniqueness follows because a prime in one factorization divides one factor in another, making them associates; cancel and induct.]

#theorem[Gauss's lemma][For a UFD $R$, a polynomial is primitive when no prime divides all its coefficients. Products of primitive polynomials are primitive. A primitive polynomial factors over the fraction field only if it factors over $R$ into polynomials of the same positive degrees. Consequently $R[x]$ is a UFD.]
#proof[If a prime divided all coefficients of a product of primitive polynomials, reducing modulo that prime would give a product of two nonzero polynomials equal to zero over the domain $R slash (p)$, impossible. Every polynomial over the fraction field is a scalar times a primitive polynomial over $R$, by clearing denominators and removing common prime factors of coefficients. For a factorization of a primitive polynomial, write both factors this way. The product of the primitive parts is primitive, so comparison of the valuation of each prime in all coefficients forces their scalar product to be a unit of $R$. Absorb that unit into one factor. Factorization over the fraction field, which is a PID polynomial ring, together with factorization of coefficient contents in $R$, now gives existence and uniqueness in $R[x]$.]

#theorem[Eisenstein's criterion][Let $f=a_n x^n+dots+a_0 in ZZ[x]$ be primitive. If a prime $p$ divides every $a_i$ for $i<n$, does not divide $a_n$, and $p^2$ does not divide $a_0$, then $f$ is irreducible over $QQ$.]
#proof[By Gauss, a rational factorization yields $f=g h$ in $ZZ[x]$ with positive degrees. Reduction modulo $p$ gives a nonzero scalar multiple of $x^n$, so both reductions are monomials of their original positive degrees (leading coefficients remain nonzero). Both constant terms are divisible by $p$, contradicting $p^2$ not dividing $a_0$.]

#example[Two tests with different scopes][$x^3-2$ is Eisenstein at two. The polynomial $x^2+1$ is irreducible over $QQ$ because it has no rational root, but it is reducible over $CC$. A root test suffices for degrees two and three; degree four can factor into two quadratics without a root.]

#example[Why “no roots” is not a general irreducibility proof][The polynomial $(x^2+1)(x^2+2)$ has no rational root, since both quadratic factors are positive at every rational input, but it is visibly reducible over $QQ$. Its degree is four. For a polynomial of degree two or three, any nontrivial factorization must include a linear factor, so absence of roots is sufficient there.]

When choosing an irreducibility method, first record the degree and coefficient ring. Try a root test in low degree, reduction modulo a prime when the degree survives, or Eisenstein after a useful translation. Failure of one sufficient criterion is not evidence of reducibility; it only means that particular test did not decide the question.

#checkpoint("6.2", [Why does an irreducible reduction modulo $p$ imply a primitive integer polynomial is irreducible over $QQ$, provided its degree is preserved?], [Gauss turns a rational factorization into an integer one. Preservation of the total degree ensures neither leading coefficient vanishes modulo $p$, so both positive-degree factors remain positive-degree factors of the reduction.])

